ID: [Enter ID number]

General Project Description
---------------------------
SkyShield is a 2D arcade game written in C++20 using the SFML 3.0 graphics
library. The player controls a drone flying over a battlefield and must
defend a stationary Command Vehicle in the center of the screen from waves
of enemies that advance toward it from left and right, and fire shells at it.
The player drops bombs on the enemies from above to destroy them before the
vehicle is wrecked.

Each level has a number of enemies that must be eliminated; once all of
them are destroyed, you win. A level ends in a loss if the vehicle is
destroyed, if the drone's battery runs out, or if all bombs are used up
and no more bombs are still falling in mid-air while enemies remain.

Each defeated enemy awards Coins that, if the level has been completed,
persist between levels and game sessions, and can be spent in the Shop
on alternative characters, backgrounds, background music, and fonts.
Some shop vehicles and drones also provide numeric bonuses - for example
improved armor for the command vehicle or larger battery capacity for the
drone - so purchases are not purely cosmetic.

The game includes a Start screen, Game screen, Shop screen, Help screen,
and Settings screen with volume and mute controls. All persistent data
(coins, owned items, selected items, audio settings) is saved to a JSON
file and reloaded on the next launch.

Note: although SFML supports fullscreen, the game runs in a regular window
from the start as recommended in the project document, so there are no
display issues over Zoom.


File Structure - List of files we created
------------------------------------------
The project is organized in a clearly modular architecture. Each module
lives in its own folder, with declarations (include/) separated from
implementation (src/), and a separation between infrastructure/Resources,
game logic (Gameplay), screens (Screens), and the overall application
(App).

src/main.cpp
~~~~~~~~~~~~
- main.cpp                 - Entry point. Creates a Game object, runs
                             run(), and catches global exceptions.

App - General application layer
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- App/Game.h / Game.cpp        - The game's main class. Manages the SFML
                                 window, the main game loop (events ->
                                 commands -> update -> render), screen
                                 transitions, loading saved data, and
                                 fatal-mode handling (runtime exceptions).
- App/State/GameState.h / .cpp - Shared game state across all screens and
                                 objects: coins, battery, available bombs,
                                 damage taken by the command vehicle,
                                 enemies remaining, level coins, owned
                                 and selected shop items, audio settings.
                                 It is passed by reference to anyone who
                                 needs to read or update it.
- App/Constants.h              - General constants (window size, base
                                 speeds, etc.).
- App/ScreenConstants.h        - Graphical layout constants for screens.
- App/AssetConstants.h         - Asset names and constants (animations,
                                 frame sizes).
- App/ButtonStyles.h / .cpp    - Shared TextButton color and metric
                                 presets used by start, shop, settings,
                                 and overlay screens.

Gameplay - Game logic
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Gameplay/World.h / World.cpp - The "engine" of a single level. Holds the
                                 list of all live objects as a vector of
                                 unique_ptr<GameObject>, drives spawning,
                                 update, collision and cleanup every
                                 frame, and checks for win/loss.
- Gameplay/Factory.h / .cpp    - Singleton Factory for creating game
                                 objects from an enum. Two lookup tables:
                                 one for regular objects and one for
                                 enemies which require additional
                                 references (drone and vehicle).
- Gameplay/CollisionHandling.h / .cpp - Multiple-dispatch implementation
                                 via a std::map<pair<type_index,type_index>,
                                 function>. Each colliding pair of types
                                 is registered in the lookup with the
                                 lambda that handles it. Pairs not in the
                                 table simply ignore each other - they
                                 do not collide.
- Gameplay/Coin.h / Coin.cpp   - HUD widget: a spinning coin icon + the
                                 coin count with a "pop" animation when
                                 the number increases.

Gameplay/Objects - Polymorphic object hierarchy
- GameObject.h / .cpp     - Abstract base for every game object. Inherits
                            from two interfaces: IDrawable and IUpdatable.
                            Absorbs all shared API: sprite, position,
                            collision bounds, alive/dead state, and the
                            spawning of objects this one wants to give
                            birth to (takeSpawn).
- MovingObject.h / .cpp   - Abstract base for moving objects. Adds
                            velocity on top of the base.
- CommandVehicle.h / .cpp - The stationary command vehicle the player
                            protects. Animated (beacon animation in
                            normal state, burning animation when in
                            critical state), shakes when hit.
- Drone.h / .cpp          - The player's drone. Reads keyboard input,
                            drops bombs, manages ammo state (available
                            bombs).
- Explosion.h / .cpp      - One-shot visual effect: plays an animation
                            and dies. Doesn't collide with anything.

Gameplay/Enemies - Enemies
- Enemy.h / Enemy.cpp     - Abstract base for all enemies. Contains the
                            entire shared AI: deciding between Advancing,
                            Firing, and Panic states, managing the fire
                            timer, U-turn flee behavior, and firing based
                            on a muzzle offset. Receives live references
                            to the drone and the vehicle, so there is no
                            need to "feed" it their positions every frame.
- FastEnemy.h / .cpp      - Fast and fragile enemy: dies from a single
                            bomb hit.
- TankEnemy.h / .cpp      - Tank enemy: two hits. The first hit only
                            wounds it (color changes), the second kills.
                            It also has a unique 5-frame firing animation
                            synchronized with the telegraph timer.

Gameplay/Projectiles - Projectiles
- Bomb.h / Bomb.cpp       - A bomb dropped by the drone. Falls straight
                            down, dies on hit or off-screen. Registers
                            itself as "in flight" so World can tell
                            whether the level is still winnable.
- Bullet.h / Bullet.cpp   - A bullet fired by an enemy at the command
                            vehicle. Horizontal movement. Dies on hit or
                            off-screen.

Graphics
~~~~~~~~
- Graphics/Animation.h / .cpp - Helper class to slice sprite sheets into
                                frames and advance cyclic animation.

Interfaces - Abstract interfaces
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- IDrawable.h    - Things that can be drawn to the screen.
- IUpdatable.h   - Things that update in real time based on dt.

Resources - Resource management (Singletons)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- ResourceManager.h / .cpp - Singleton that lazily loads textures and
                             fonts and stores them in a map. Supports
                             runtime swapping of assets based on shop
                             selections.
- SoundManager.h / .cpp    - Singleton for playing background music and
                             effects, with volume and mute controls.
- DataManager.h / .cpp     - Singleton that reads gameData.json (level
                             config + shop catalog) and saves/loads
                             userData (user state). Uses nlohmann::json.
- Resources.h              - Type/alias definitions for resources.

Shop
~~~~
- Shop/ShopData.h / .cpp    - Mappings between the shop-item-type enum
                              and strings appearing in JSON.
- Shop/ShopManager.h / .cpp - Shop logic: hover, active category, opening
                              the preview, purchase and selection.

Screens - Application screens (Strategy/State hierarchy)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Screens/Core/Screen.h / .cpp - Abstract base for any screen. Game holds
                                 a single Screen pointer and swaps the
                                 object itself on screen transitions.
                                 Each screen can emit a ScreenCommand
                                 (Quit, ToShop, etc.) that Game handles.
- Screens/StartScreen.h / .cpp - Start screen with Play, Shop, Settings,
                                 Help, Exit buttons.
- Screens/GameScreen.h / .cpp  - The actual game screen. Holds World,
                                 manages pause/continue buttons, shows
                                 HUD (battery, bombs, coins), and shows
                                 win/loss overlay at level end.
- Screens/ShopScreen.h / .cpp  - The shop screen. Wraps ShopManager +
                                 drawing.
- Screens/HelpScreen.h / .cpp  - Help screen explaining game rules,
                                 controls, and win/loss conditions
                                 (explicit project requirement).
- Screens/SettingsScreen.h / .cpp        - Settings screen UI. Emits
                                           settings actions and volume
                                           changes without saving directly.
- Screens/SettingsScreenManager.h / .cpp - Wraps SettingsScreen, applies
                                           settings actions, saves user
                                           data, and opens/closes the Help
                                           screen from Settings.
- Screens/Components/Menubar.h / .cpp           - Shared top menu bar.
- Screens/Components/OverlayView.h / .cpp       - Generic overlay usable
                                                  for modal messages.
- Screens/Components/ScrollableTextOverlay.h / .cpp - Scrollable text
                                                  overlay.
- Screens/Components/ShopPreview.h / .cpp       - Preview of a shop item.
- Screens/Components/TextButton.h / .cpp        - Reusable button component
                                                  with hover, selected,
                                                  disabled, callback, and
                                                  text-fitting behavior.

Structs - POD data structures also represented in JSON
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- GameData.h              - Everything loaded from gameData.json: list of
                            levels + shop catalog.
- LevelConfig.h           - Level parameters: enemy count, tank
                            percentage, spawn interval, battery drain
                            rate, starting bomb count.
- UserData.h              - Persistent user data: coins, owned items,
                            selected items, mute and volume.
- ShopAsset.h             - One item in the shop catalog.
- EnemyTypeConfig.h       - Enemy parameters: speed, reward, sprite,
                            muzzle offset.
- AnimatedSpriteConfig.h  - Sprite sheet description (frames, dimensions).
- MuzzleOffset.h          - Muzzle position relative to the sprite center.
- ResourceInfo.h          - Metadata for a resource.
- OverlayAction.h         - An action invokable from an overlay.
- ScreenEventContext.h    - Event context (mouse position in view, etc.)
                            a screen receives without window access.
- TextButtonColors.h      - Color set for the reusable TextButton states.
- TextButtonMetrics.h     - Sizing, padding, outline, and fitting settings
                            for TextButton.

Enums
~~~~~
- Enums/GameObjects.h     - Game object types (Background, Vehicle, Drone,
                            Bomb, Bullet, Explosion, etc.).
- Enums/EnemyType.h       - Fast / Tank.
- Enums/EnemyState.h      - Advancing / Firing / Panic.
- Enums/ActiveScreen.h    - Which screen is currently active.
- Enums/ScreenCommand.h   - Commands a screen can request from Game
                            (Quit, ReturnToStart, OpenShop, StartGame,
                            etc.).
- Enums/LevelResult.h     - Running / Won / Lost.
- Enums/ShopAssetType.h   - Shop categories.
- Enums/ShopClickResult.h - Result of a click in the shop screen.
- Enums/PreviewAction.h   - Preview action (Buy/Select/Close).
- Enums/OverlayActionType.h - Types of overlay buttons.
- Enums/StartScreenButton.h - Start screen buttons.
- Enums/SettingsAction.h  - Actions requested by the settings screen.
- Enums/SettingsControl.h - Clickable controls in the settings screen.
- Enums/Fonts.h           - Font identifiers.
- Enums/Sounds.h          - Sound identifiers.

Utilities
~~~~~~~~~
- Utilities/Utils.h / .cpp       - General helper namespaces for random
                                   numbers, geometry checks, drawing setup,
                                   and text formatting.
- Utilities/AppUtils.h / .cpp    - Application helpers (initial window
                                   size and matching start-screen
                                   backgrounds).

external
~~~~~~~~
- external/json.hpp       - nlohmann::json library (single header). Not
                            written by us, only integrated.

Packages
~~~~~~~~
- SFML 3.0                - Window, graphics, audio, and input package.
- nlohmann::json          - Header-only package used only for reading and
                            writing JSON files.

resources
~~~~~~~~~
- data/gameData.json      - Configuration for all levels and the shop.
                            This file can be edited to add levels, change
                            difficulty, or add shop items without
                            recompiling.
- images/                 - Images (default + shop assets).
- sounds/                 - Music and sound effects.
- fonts/                  - Fonts.


Main Data Structures and Their Roles
-------------------------------------
- std::vector<std::unique_ptr<GameObject>> in the World class
  The main vector that owns all live objects in a level. A vector of
  unique_ptr to an abstract base allows uniform polymorphic handling of
  every object type (vehicle, drone, enemies, bombs, bullets, explosions)
  - they are all updated in a single for-each loop, threaded together
  for collision checks, and removed together via std::remove_if. The
  unique_ptr guarantees there is no memory leak even when an object dies
  mid-frame: the vector is solely responsible for lifetime.

- std::map<std::pair<std::type_index, std::type_index>, std::function<...>>
  in the collision mechanism. This is a multiple-dispatch lookup: a pair
  of dynamic types -> a handler that handles the collision between them.
  Instead of branching if-else over object types, we look in the table
  and if something is there, we invoke it. Unregistered pairs (bomb+bomb,
  bullet+enemy, etc.) simply ignore each other. Extending the code with
  a new collision type = one new line in the map.

- std::map<GameObjects, CreatorFunc> and std::map<EnemyType, EnemyCreatorFunc>
  in Factory. Two lookup tables for building objects from an enum, using
  std::function. Allows World to construct a "generic" object without
  knowing exactly which one - it requests by enum and Factory handles
  instantiation, accesses the "right" texture via ResourceManager, etc.

- std::map<ShopAssetType, std::set<int>> in GameState (owned items)
  and std::map<ShopAssetType, int> (the currently selected item per
  category, and its numeric extra). The set enables O(log n) membership
  testing of an owned item, and the per-category map makes the current
  selection efficient and trivial to serialize.

- std::unique_ptr<Screen> in the Game class
  A single pointer to an abstract base representing the active screen.
  Swapping a screen = creating a new unique_ptr and assigning it - the
  previous screen is destroyed automatically. State/Strategy architecture.


Algorithms Worth Mentioning
----------------------------
1. Multiple dispatch (double dispatch) for collisions
   Instead of a double virtual call or a visitor pattern, we used dynamic
   typeid to produce a std::type_index and looked the handler up in a
   map. This makes extension very easy: adding a new colliding object
   requires only one line in the table, with no changes to existing code.
   For symmetric pairs (e.g. Bomb+FastEnemy and FastEnemy+Bomb), both
   directions are registered to avoid relying on argument order.

2. Enemy AI: a small state machine + telegraph + bracketing
   Each enemy is in one of three states: Advancing (moves toward the
   vehicle), Firing (stops and shoots), Panic (flees when the drone is
   above it). State transitions are range-based: distance to the vehicle
   triggers firing, distance to the drone triggers panic. The firing
   state has a "telegraph" - a time window where the enemy stops and
   tells the player it is about to fire (TankEnemy plays a 5-frame
   wind-up animation) before the bullet is actually released, giving the
   player a chance to react. The decision whether to advance or flee
   uses calculateWalls, which computes movement bounds (screen edges +
   vehicle position), and updateUTurnState, which prevents jitter when
   flight in one direction is blocked.

3. Level coin staging and commit-on-win
   Enemy rewards are first accumulated in GameState as levelCoins. They
   are displayed in the HUD and level summary, but are added to the saved
   coin balance only by commitLevelCoins after a level is won. This keeps
   failed attempts from permanently granting coins, and the commit flag
   prevents the same level reward from being added twice.

4. Live asset swapping based on the shop catalog
   applyUserDataSelections iterates over the JSON catalog, cross-references
   with the user's selections in userData, and loads the matching
   texture/music/font at runtime while swapping the entry in the
   Resources map. This means changing a drone or vehicle "skin" requires
   no recompile or restart - it is immediate.

5. Targeted userData saving
   User data is saved only in flows that actually change persistent
   state: after winning a level, changing settings, resetting user data,
   or buying/selecting shop items. Game only loads the saved data at
   startup and applies the current selections.

6. Safety management for bombs in mid-air
   World cannot declare a "no bombs left" loss while a bomb is still
   falling, because that bomb can still kill an enemy. Therefore Bomb
   registers an activeBombs counter in GameState in its constructor and
   decrements it in its destructor. The isLossConditionMet check
   accounts for this.


Game Rules, Controls and Detailed Interaction Breakdown
--------------------------------------------------------
Controls
~~~~~~~~
- Left arrow / Right arrow : Move the drone left / right.
- Space bar                : Drop a bomb (edge-triggered - one press =
                             one bomb, must release and re-press to drop
                             another).
- Escape                   : Pause / go back in menu screens.
- Mouse                    : Navigation in every screen that isn't the
                             game itself, and clicking HUD buttons in
                             the game (Pause).

What kills what (detailed interaction breakdown)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Bomb -> FastEnemy:
  The bomb detonates and dies. The fast enemy dies instantly from one
  hit. An Explosion is spawned at the location, and we receive the
  reward defined in the EnemyTypeConfig for FastEnemy.

- Bomb -> TankEnemy:
  The bomb detonates and dies. The tank - on the first hit only
  "wounded" (m_wounded becomes true and it changes color), but still
  alive and continuing to move and fire. Only on the second hit does
  it die, and we receive TankEnemy's full reward.

- Bullet -> CommandVehicle:
  The bullet detonates and dies. The vehicle takes damage - takeDamage()
  reduces the armor percentage by the configured damage, but if the
  player has purchased an "Upgraded Command Vehicle" (or another item
  with an extra > 0 field), the effective damage taken is reduced
  accordingly via adjustedVehicleDamage. When armor reaches 0%, the
  vehicle is destroyed.

- Bomb -> bullet / Bomb -> vehicle / Bomb -> drone / Bullet -> bullet /
  Bullet -> enemy / any pair not in collisionLookup:
  No collision at all. Your bomb cannot hurt your vehicle, and an enemy
  bullet is not stopped by your bomb on the way. This is intentional -
  it simplifies the logic and keeps the game fair.

- Drone: takes no direct damage from anything. It has only psychological
  pressure on the enemies (they flee from it), and the only way to lose
  through the drone is by battery depletion (drains at the level's
  batteryDrain rate) or by dropping all bombs without killing all enemies.

Win condition
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- All enemies in the level have been destroyed (m_enemiesRemaining == 0).

Loss conditions (any one of them is enough)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- The command vehicle is destroyed (armor = 0).
- The drone's battery runs out (batteryPercent = 0).
- Out of bombs (bombsAvailable = 0) AND no bombs in mid-air
  (activeBombs = 0) AND enemies still remain - the level is no longer
  winnable.

Coins and shop
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
- Every enemy destroyed = level coins based on its getReward() (defined
  in JSON).
- Level coins are committed to the saved coin balance only after winning
  the level. Coins accumulate between completed levels and between
  sessions (saved in userData.json).
- The shop has 7 categories: Command Vehicle, Drone, Fast Enemy (skin),
  Tank Enemy (skin), Background, Background Music, Font.
- For vehicles and drones, the extra field translates to a numeric bonus
  (extra armor / larger battery capacity).


OOP Techniques Applied in This Project
---------------------------------------
- Multi-layered inheritance and polymorphism:
  GameObject (abstract) -> MovingObject (abstract) -> Drone, Bomb,
  Bullet, Enemy. Enemy -> FastEnemy, TankEnemy. CommandVehicle, Explosion
  inherit directly from GameObject.
- Pure interfaces: IDrawable, IUpdatable with pure virtual functions.
- Abstraction: getSpeed, onHit, getReward, updateAnimation - virtual
  functions that FastEnemy and TankEnemy implement differently.
- RAII and unambiguous ownership: every dynamic allocation through
  unique_ptr. There is no manual delete in the entire project.
- Exceptions: thrown on resource-loading failures or malformed JSON;
  caught in main and also in the run() loop (fatal mode with overlay).
- Design patterns: Singleton (ResourceManager, SoundManager, DataManager,
  Factory), Factory, Strategy/State (Screen), Observer (not explicitly
  registered, but GameState acts as a shared message bus).
- STL: vector, map, set, unique_ptr, function, optional, pair, algorithm
  (remove_if, find_if), filesystem, chrono.
- Packages: SFML 3.0 for graphics, sound, window, and input;
  nlohmann::json for reading configuration and saving state.
- Genericity and extensibility:
  * Adding a new level = an additional entry in stages in JSON, zero
    lines of code.
  * Adding a new skin for vehicle/drone/enemy = an additional entry in
    shopAssets in JSON.
  * Adding a new enemy type = a new class inheriting from Enemy,
    registration in Factory, and registration of collision pairs in
    CollisionHandling.
  * Replacing the graphics engine = only Animation, GameObject::draw,
    and Screen. The logic isn't aware of SFML.


Known Bugs
----------
None. No known bugs at the time of submission.


Other Notes
-----------
- The game runs at 60 FPS (setFramerateLimit). All motion and animation
  logic is multiplied by dt so it is independent of frame rate.
- gameData.json is loaded once at startup. To try different levels you
  can edit the file without recompiling. The structure of a level is
  simple and documented in LevelConfig.
- Classes and public facing functions are written in a uniform style with Doxygen-style comments
  above member functions.
- Code regions are marked with #pragma region for better readability.
